{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-14_09-22-37-hashicorp_raft-real-run/draft; python3 - <<'PYEOF'\nimport json\np='update.json'\nd=json.load(open(p))\nd['knowledge']=[{\n \\\"id\\\":\\\"6420ecc11ae840028bf3e70220f5fd17-knowledge-1\\\",\n \\\"kind\\\":\\\"fact\\\",\n \\\"statement\\\":(\\\"A configuration that a node holds only in its log can be restored to that node's in-memory state by four paths, of which only one is in-protocol: a later configuration entry \\\"\n  \\\"replicated by a correctly configured leader (appendEntries processes new configuration entries); an operator restart with RestoreCommittedLogs disabled; RecoverCluster on the stopped node with the \\\"\n  \\\"configuration supplied, which writes a snapshot carrying it and truncates the log so the next NewRaft restores it from the snapshot; and an installed snapshot, whose metadata carries the configuration. \\\"\n  \\\"In the restart scenarios recorded here none of the four is triggered by the node's own restart: a leader sends a snapshot only to a follower that lacks already-compacted entries (replication.go 222-224), \\\"\n  \\\"and the affected node's log is complete, so no snapshot is sent to it and no configuration entry reaches it uncommitted. The snapshot path is nevertheless reachable for a node with a complete log whenever \\\"\n  \\\"the leader's own log has a hole above that node's next index, as after a leader-side user restore, which deliberately leaves such a hole (raft.go 1097-1199). Where every voter restarts with the flag enabled \\\"\n  \\\"and no covering snapshot, the in-protocol path needs a leader that the empty configuration prevents from being elected, so the cluster stays unavailable until an operator acts.\\\"),\n \\\"conditions\\\":(\\\"RestoreCommittedLogs=true with a CommitTrackingLogStore and configuration entries inside the replayed committed prefix. Observed with: a four-voter cluster in which a stale node is a minority \\\"\n  \\\"(in-protocol repair by a later configuration entry, and the no-repair case); three voters all restarted at once (no in-protocol repair and no leader); and a single-voter cluster in the empty-configuration \\\"\n  \\\"state for the operator paths (live bootstrap refusing, RecoverCluster succeeding, and the flag-disabled restart restoring all voters).\\\"),\n \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"newraft_scan\\\",\\\"restore_committed\\\",\\\"append_new_entries\\\",\\\"snapshot_install_config\\\",\\\"bootstrap_refusal\\\",\\\"recover_cluster\\\",\\\"snapshot_trigger\\\",\\\"user_restore_hole\\\"],\n \\\"executions\\\":{\\\"6d6abaf163524e64878c6c9810de2461\\\":[5],\\\"4f4a771f03b649c2a5703b0ff279ff3a\\\":[1],\\\"0440f054115e44f4a0234c7a4ef67150\\\":[1,2],\\\"81163da076c64417bd48e0f79260d73d\\\":[2]},\n \\\"changed_premise\\\":(\\\"Restores this Fact's own scope after a merge artifact: instead of the repair-path scope, the previous revision carried the trigger Fact's conditions (a node started on stores whose persisted commit index is at or above the newest configuration entry) \\\"\n  \\\"and had lost two source anchors and the in-protocol repair receipt. This revision states the conditions under which the four paths were observed, restores the source list including the bootstrap refusal, RecoverCluster, the snapshot trigger and the user-restore hole, \\\"\n  \\\"and restores the receipt for the in-protocol repair; the corrected four-path statement and the user-restore caveat are unchanged from the previous revision.\\\")\n}]\njson.dump(d, open(p,'w'), indent=2)\nprint('ok')\nPYEOF\ncd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-14_09-22-37-hashicorp_raft-real-run; /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --